Metal: stop sleeping a flat millisecond per command-buffer wait poll

0019 gave the command-buffer wait a deadline, which it needed, but implemented
the wait as a poll loop that sleeps a flat 1 ms per iteration. That puts a 1 ms
floor under every wait whose command buffer is not already complete.

On a 1024^2 two-species single-precision deck the launch rate is about 205
kernels per step with roughly 3.1 waits per kernel, so about 640 waits per step
reach that loop. Measured against the same deck, host, and cache with only the
runtime differing, the floor cost 36 -> 328 ms/step, a 9x wall regression; the
arithmetic closes if a little under half of those waits sleep once.

Metal command buffers on this hardware typically retire in tens of microseconds,
so the fix is to match the wait granularity to that: yield-spin first, then fall
back to exponential backoff from 2 us to a 1 ms cap for genuinely long waits so
a stalled queue does not burn a core. The deadline and every error path are
unchanged - this is purely how the loop waits between status checks.

Measured on the same deck, host, and cache:

  flat 1 ms poll            ~328 ms/step
  backoff only (2 us .. 1 ms) ~55 ms/step
  spin then backoff          ~48 ms/step
  runtime without the deadline machinery at all   ~36 ms/step

Particle counts were exact on every run in all configurations.

The residual against the no-deadline runtime is wait granularity: about 12 ms
per step over ~640 waits is ~19 us per wait. Removing it entirely would need an
event-driven wait, and Metal requires completion handlers to be registered
before commit, so that is a larger change to the submit path rather than to
this function.

diff --git a/src/runtime/metal/metal_queue.cpp b/src/runtime/metal/metal_queue.cpp
index 39e0786..5fc8a12 100644
--- a/src/runtime/metal/metal_queue.cpp
+++ b/src/runtime/metal/metal_queue.cpp
@@ -428,6 +428,18 @@ result wait_for_metal_command_buffer(MTL::CommandBuffer* command_buffer,
   const auto deadline =
       std::chrono::steady_clock::now() + metal_command_buffer_timeout();
   auto status = command_buffer->status();
+  // Back off from 2 us up to 1 ms instead of sleeping a flat 1 ms per poll.
+  // Derivation: a flat 1 ms floor costs ~1 ms on EVERY wait that is not already
+  // complete. At ~205 launches/step and ~3.1 waits/kernel that is ~640 waits/step;
+  // if ~46% of them sleep once, that is ~292 ms/step of pure sleep, which is what
+  // we measured: 36 -> 328 ms/step on a 1024^2 PPC1 deck (a 9x wall regression).
+  // Metal command buffers typically retire in tens of microseconds, so starting at
+  // 2 us and doubling keeps the common case in the microsecond range, while the
+  // 1 ms cap bounds wakeups for genuinely long waits. Deadline semantics unchanged.
+  auto backoff = std::chrono::microseconds{2};
+  const auto max_backoff = std::chrono::microseconds{1000};
+  int spins = 0;
+  constexpr int max_spins = 256;
   while (status != MTL::CommandBufferStatusCompleted &&
          status != MTL::CommandBufferStatusError) {
     if (std::chrono::steady_clock::now() >= deadline) {
@@ -438,7 +450,22 @@ result wait_for_metal_command_buffer(MTL::CommandBuffer* command_buffer,
               << metal_command_buffer_status_name(status);
       return make_error(__acpp_here(), error_info{message.str()});
     }
-    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds{1});
+    // Phase 1: yield-spin. Measured residual under pure backoff was ~30 us/wait
+    // (19.4 ms/step over ~640 waits/step), which is just the 2+4+8+16 geometric
+    // sum needed to cover a typical ~30 us command-buffer retirement. A yield
+    // costs well under a microsecond, so ~256 of them cover that same window at
+    // far finer granularity than a 2 us sleep floor can. Phase 2: fall back to
+    // the exponential backoff for genuinely long waits so we do not burn a core.
+    if (spins < max_spins) {
+      ++spins;
+      std::this_thread::yield();
+    } else {
+      std::this_thread::sleep_for(backoff);
+      if (backoff < max_backoff) {
+        backoff *= 2;
+        if (backoff > max_backoff) { backoff = max_backoff; }
+      }
+    }
     status = command_buffer->status();
   }
 
